// BackendDispatch — the Phase-7 backend driver: it takes a lowered StdModule to
// a runnable executable. `buildBackend` runs the fixed Std→Target→CodeResult
// sequence; `augmentWithRuntime` supplies the OS entry point; `writeExecutable`
// dispatches the CodeResult to the per-OS object writer.
//
// Std, not MIR: shv2 has no MIR tier (see StdDialect's header). The backend's
// input is the Std module the pipeline produced, and `lowerStdToX64` asserts on
// entry that no Std-only `sugar` op survived to reach it.
//
// These stages are driven as ordered function calls rather than PassKind entries
// (the pipeline in PassPipeline stops at StdModule): the backend produces a
// CodeResult, which is not an IR module, so it does not fit the module-field
// dispatch model. This mirrors v1, where Phase 7 is backend-direct.
//
// THIN for maxon-shv2 M1 (x64-windows only). `augmentWithRuntime` installs the
// `mrt_start` entry stub; the PANIC RUNTIME it calls into (the `#DE` fault handler
// and its symbolized backtrace) is appended as hand-assembled chunks during the
// encode phase — see `buildBackend` and X64Runtime's header for why it cannot be
// TargetOps. A `return 42` program still allocates nothing and spawns nothing, so it
// carries none of the REST of v1's unconditional x64-windows runtime floor (no
// slab/MM init, no green-thread scheduler, no IOCP). Both halves are designed to
// GROW: M6 prepends slab/MM init, Phase F the green-thread scheduler.
// Linux/macOS/wasi entry stubs + `writeElf`/`writeMachO` dispatch arrive at their
// milestones.

// The user program's entry function — `mrt_start` calls it, then bridges its
// R8 return value to ExitProcess.
export let UserEntryName = "main"

// `mrt_start`'s frame: 32 bytes of Win64 shadow space for the calls it makes, and
// NOTHING else. The OS enters the stub with rsp ≡ 8 (mod 16), as if via `call`; the
// stub's own `push rbp` moves it to ≡ 0, so an exact-multiple-of-16 reservation keeps
// it aligned at each `call` and no odd 8 is needed.
//
// ⚠ This USED to be 0x28 (32 + 8) — the 8 was the alignment correction for a stub that
// pushed nothing. The frame pointer's push now supplies that parity, so the same
// arithmetic `computeFrameBytes` does for every other function (entry ≡ 8, one push ⇒ an
// even reservation) gives 0x20 here. Keeping 0x28 would leave rsp ≡ 8 AT the call — a
// misalignment nothing on this path reads today, and that would start corrupting the
// moment anything below it spills an XMM register (P1.0d.4 floats).
let EntryStubFrameBytes = 0x20

// Run the full backend: Std → Target → register allocation → prologue/epilogue →
// runtime augmentation → machine-code emission → CodeResult.
//
// `throws CompileError` because TWO backend stages can FAIL for a real reason the user must fix, both
// reported onto `project.diagnostics`: register allocation raises E5001 when a loop needs more registers
// than exist, and the reachability prune raises E4015 when a declaration collides with a symbol the
// compiler emits (A1t — the first stage that can see BOTH, so the first that can tell them apart).
// `project` also carries the `opRanges`/`valueOrigins` tables the allocator maps a Target ValueId back to
// its source span through. Every other backend stage is infallible — a failure there is a compiler bug and
// still panics.
// Each stage below is bracketed by one `PhaseProbe`, which measures its wall time AND its
// allocations at the same two instants (see PhaseProbe's header for why that is one type
// and not two). The stages are strictly sequential and none calls another, so the phases
// are disjoint by construction and nothing has to be subtracted back out (unlike the
// allocator's internals, which nest — see CompileTimings' header). `regalloc`'s own seven
// sub-phases are recorded into `project.timings.regAlloc` / `project.memory.regAlloc` and
// nest UNDER this phase in both reports; they are a breakdown of what is measured here,
// never additional phases.
// Run the backend for `target`, dispatched on its CPU: the x64 pipeline, or the arm64 one
// (`Arm64Backend`, wave 1). Both share the register allocator, the runtime augmentation and the
// per-OS writer; they differ in isel + encoder + frame, which is exactly the per-ISA split the
// unified `TargetOp` was chosen to allow (USER RULING). The sugar gate is target-neutral and runs
// here, once, so every backend inherits it.
//
// ⭐ **`usage` IS THE INSTALLATION DECISION, CARRIED — NOT RE-DERIVED.** Three things down here are
// conditional on which emitted runtime the program carries: the entry stub's leak gate, the per-function
// green-thread stack guard, and the hand-assembled GT/IOCP chunks. All three are part of an
// INSTALLATION, and installation is decided exactly once, before lowering, by `RuntimeUsage` — see
// DeadFunctionElimination's header for the full split of duties.
//
// ⚠ They used to ask the MODULE instead ("does it contain `__mm_leak_check` / `__gt_spawn` /
// `__gt_io_read`?"), on the argument that by this point the module IS the answer, so a threaded flag
// would be the same fact written down twice and free to disagree. That argument rested on a premise
// DEAD-FUNCTION ELIMINATION removes: that nothing takes an installed function back out. It does, and a
// probe for one representative function is not a probe for the runtime it represents — a `sleep(1)`
// program installs the whole GT scheduler and never calls `__gt_spawn`, so pruning that one function
// (correctly) turned the chunk gate off and the link failed on `__gt_context_switch`, which nine
// async/streaming specs caught at once. The fact is now written down ONCE, where it is decided, and
// read here.
export function buildBackend(stdModule StdModule, target Target, globalData GlobalDataTable, project Project, usage RuntimeUsage) returns CodeResult throws CompileError
	let sugarProbe = PhaseProbe.beginPhase(project, phase: CompilePhase.sugarGate)
	assertNoSugarOps(stdModule)
	recordPhase(project, phase: CompilePhase.sugarGate, probe: sugarProbe)

	// Prune what cannot be reached BEFORE the expensive half — isel, register allocation, frames,
	// encoding and linking all run per function, so a function dropped here is work no backend does.
	// Target-neutral and therefore HERE rather than inside a backend, for the same reason the sugar gate
	// is: reachability is a property of the Std tier, so x64/arm64/wasm inherit it instead of each
	// re-adding it. `globalData` reaches it because an `.rdata` slot is a reference this module's ops do
	// not spell (see DeadFunctionElimination's root set).
	//
	// It `throws` for one reason, and it is a USER error rather than a pruning failure: this is the LAST site
	// that builds a `FunctionNameIndex` over a module, and the only one downstream of every install — so a
	// declaration colliding with a symbol the compiler emits is visible here and nowhere earlier, and E4015 is
	// reported at the index. Its root set raises the same code for the other half of that rule (a name the
	// compiler ROOTS, in a program that installs nothing under it) — see `markCompilerOwnedRoot`.
	let dfeProbe = PhaseProbe.beginPhase(project, phase: CompilePhase.deadFunctions)
	// ⭐⭐ **THE ONE NAME INDEX EVERY BACKEND RESOLVES A CALLEE THROUGH (A2l).** It comes back from the
	// prune rather than being built here because the prune is what makes an earlier one stale, and it is
	// built at all because NOTHING APPENDS A FUNCTION AFTER THIS LINE: every `install*Runtime` ran in
	// `compileToCodeResult` above, the entry stub and the hand-assembled runtime chunks join at the TARGET
	// tier as chunks rather than Std functions, and this dispatch is the last thing between the prune and
	// the backends. So an index taken here describes the array all three backends will walk — which is what
	// lets their per-callee maps take the INDEX as an argument instead of the module, and stop resting on a
	// uniqueness guarantee none of them stated.
	let funcNames = try eliminateDeadFunctions(stdModule, globalData: globalData, project: project)
	recordPhase(project, phase: CompilePhase.deadFunctions, probe: dfeProbe)

	match target.cpu 'cpu'
		x64 then return try buildX64Backend(stdModule, target: target, globalData: globalData, project: project, usage: usage, funcNames: funcNames)
		arm64 then return try buildArm64Backend(stdModule, target: target, globalData: globalData, project: project, usage: usage, funcNames: funcNames)
		wasm32 then return buildWasmBackend(stdModule, globalData: globalData, project: project, usage: usage, funcNames: funcNames)
	end 'cpu'
end 'buildBackend'

// The wasm backend: Std → a WASI-Preview2 CORE module (bytes). Unlike x64/arm64 it consumes the
// StdModule DIRECTLY — no register allocation, no prologue/epilogue, no Target dialect (see
// StdToWasm's header) — so this backend's isel, encode and link work is fused into the single
// `buildWasmCoreModule` call and billed together to `encode`. The `$run` entry (and the `wasi:cli/exit`
// import it calls) are synthesized inside that call, so `augmentWithRuntime` is NOT run for wasm and
// its `wasi` arm stays unreached. `globalData` reaches it because a top-level `var` is a `.data` slot
// this backend lays into an active linear-memory segment and resolves every `globalAddr` against —
// the same laid-out table the native writers serialize into their `.data` section. The bytes ride in
// the CodeResult's `wasmCoreModule` field; every machine-code field is empty (see
// `CodeResult.forWasm`), and `writeWasm` wraps the bytes into a component. It takes no `target`: the
// sole wasm target is wasm32-wasi, and `buildBackend` has already dispatched on it.
// `usage` reaches it for the ONE reason the native backends need it: the leak gate. `usesHeap` is
// where "was the MM runtime installed?" is decided, so wasm reads that bit rather than re-deriving it
// from the module — see `StdToWasm.appendLeakGate`.
function buildWasmBackend(module StdModule, globalData GlobalDataTable, project Project, usage RuntimeUsage, funcNames FunctionNameIndex) returns CodeResult
	let encodeProbe = PhaseProbe.beginPhase(project, phase: CompilePhase.encode)
	let coreModule = buildWasmCoreModule(module, globalData: globalData, checkLeaks: usage.usesHeap, initGlobals: usage.usesManagedGlobals, funcNames: funcNames)
	recordPhase(project, phase: CompilePhase.encode, probe: encodeProbe)

	return CodeResult.forWasm(module, wasmCoreModule: coreModule)
end 'buildWasmBackend'

// The x64 backend: Std → Target → register allocation → prologue/epilogue → runtime augmentation →
// machine-code emission → CodeResult.
function buildX64Backend(stdModule StdModule, target Target, globalData GlobalDataTable, project Project, usage RuntimeUsage, funcNames FunctionNameIndex) returns CodeResult throws CompileError
	let iselProbe = PhaseProbe.beginPhase(project, phase: CompilePhase.isel)
	// `globalData` reaches ISel because a float CONSTANT is `.rdata` content the lowering is the
	// first to see (`lowerFloatConst`). It is the same table concat resolves those labels against
	// once sections are laid out — see `lowerStdToX64`.
	// THE CLASS COLUMN CROSSES THE TIER BOUNDARY HERE, and it travels FORWARD, WITH the module: this
	// call is the last pass that can see a Std type, so it is the only one that can say which register
	// FILE a value lives in. The allocator consumes that answer and never re-derives it — `movRegReg`,
	// `loadRegSlot` and `xchgRegReg` are class-agnostic by design, so a copy's class is knowable only
	// from its source (see TargetDialect's `ValueClassColumn`).
	//
	// The two come back TOGETHER because a `TargetModule` alone is not a complete answer: it carries no
	// types, so nothing downstream could say what file any of its values belongs in. Returning the pair
	// is what makes it impossible to hand the allocator one without the other.
	var (targetModule, valueClasses) = lowerStdToX64(stdModule, target: target, globalData: globalData, funcNames: funcNames)
	recordPhase(project, phase: CompilePhase.isel, probe: iselProbe)

	let regAllocProbe = PhaseProbe.beginPhase(project, phase: CompilePhase.regalloc)
	try allocateRegisters(targetModule, target: target, project: project, valueClasses: valueClasses)
	recordPhase(project, phase: CompilePhase.regalloc, probe: regAllocProbe)

	// EC11, and it must run HERE — after the allocator's `applyAllocation` and before the frame pass.
	// A block whose only op is a `jmp` is genuinely empty only once SSA destruction has placed (or
	// declined to place) its edge copies; before that it is an edge about to receive moves. The frame
	// pass then sees the surviving blocks, so a callee-saved register or a `call` reachable only from
	// dead code stops being paid for. See `X64BranchCleanup`'s header.
	cleanUpX64Branches(targetModule, project: project)

	let prologueProbe = PhaseProbe.beginPhase(project, phase: CompilePhase.prologueEpilogue)
	insertPrologueEpilogue(targetModule, target: target)
	recordPhase(project, phase: CompilePhase.prologueEpilogue, probe: prologueProbe)

	let runtimeProbe = PhaseProbe.beginPhase(project, phase: CompilePhase.runtimeAugment)
	// The stub's two producer calls ride the module's own armed flag (`buildCarriesDebugStream`, set at the
	// install seam), never a second spelling of that conjunction: a stub calling a `__ds_init` the module
	// never installed does not link.
	augmentWithRuntime(targetModule, target: target, checkLeaks: usage.usesHeap, initGlobals: usage.usesManagedGlobals, drainGreenThreads: usage.usesGt, debugStream: stdModule.debugStream)
	recordPhase(project, phase: CompilePhase.runtimeAugment, probe: runtimeProbe)

	let encodeProbe = PhaseProbe.beginPhase(project, phase: CompilePhase.encode)
	var chunks = FunctionCodeChunkArray.create()
	for func in targetModule.functions 'eachFunc'
		chunks.push(emitFunctionChunk(targetModule, func: func, usesGt: usage.usesGt))
	end 'eachFunc'
	// The panic runtime is hand-assembled bytes, not TargetOps, so it joins here rather than
	// in `augmentWithRuntime` — there is no Std or Target spelling of "read CONTEXT.Rip" or
	// "walk a saved-RBP chain" at this milestone (see X64Runtime's header). It appends AFTER
	// user code, which keeps `mrt_start` at `.text` offset 0 where the backtrace needs it.
	//
	// x64-windows installs a Windows Vectored Exception Handler; x64-linux installs a SIGFPE handler via
	// rt_sigaction (`appendX64RuntimeChunks` branches on the OS). Both catch the `idiv` #DE and turn it
	// into a panic diagnostic + symbolized backtrace + exit 1; only the Windows one can say WHICH #DE
	// (a zero divisor vs `i64.min / -1`), because Linux reports one `si_code` for both — see
	// `X64Runtime.buildLinuxFaultHandlerChunk`. The arm64 backends instead define `sdiv`-by-0 as 0, and
	// AArch64 does not trap on an unrepresentable quotient either, so they need no fault handler.
	// macos/wasi never reach here for an x64 module: `augmentWithRuntime` above already rejected them.
	match target.os 'panicRuntime'
		windows to linux then appendX64RuntimeChunks(chunks, target: target)
		macos to wasi then panic("buildX64Backend: augmentWithRuntime should have rejected {targetToString(target)} before the encode phase")
	end 'panicRuntime'
	// The three hand-assembled green-thread chunks (`__gt_context_switch`, `__gt_trampoline`,
	// `__gt_morestack`) and, under `usesIocp`, the IOCP completion-drain loop are part of the GT
	// runtime's INSTALLATION, so they ride the same bits `installGtRuntime` was gated on rather than a
	// probe of the module (see this file's `buildBackend` header for the probe that DFE invalidated).
	if usage.usesGt 'gtChunks'
		appendX64GtRuntimeChunks(chunks, usesIocp: usage.usesIocp)
	end 'gtChunks'
	recordPhase(project, phase: CompilePhase.encode, probe: encodeProbe)

	let linkProbe = PhaseProbe.beginPhase(project, phase: CompilePhase.link)
	let codeResult = try concatX64FunctionChunks(targetModule, stdModule: stdModule, chunks: chunks, globalData: globalData, project: project, funcNames: funcNames)
	recordPhase(project, phase: CompilePhase.link, probe: linkProbe)

	return codeResult
end 'buildX64Backend'

// Function/chunk name → the byte offset that chunk starts at in `.text`. Declared ONCE, here, because
// it is one fact: x64 and arm64 each carried an identical typealias under its own name, and both concat
// passes key exactly this map with exactly this meaning.
export typealias FuncLabelOffsetMap = Map with (ByteArray, CodeOffset)

// How many labels a concat pass has laid so far — the ordinal `recordChunkLabel` compares the map's height
// against. Not a `FuncIndex`: a chunk is not a module function (the entry stub, the hand-assembled runtime
// chunks and `__symtable` have no position in `stdModule.functions`).
module typealias ChunkLabelCount = int(0 to u64.max)

// ⭐⭐ **LAY ONE CHUNK'S LABEL DOWN, AND REFUSE A SECOND CLAIM ON THAT NAME (A2l).**
//
// ⚠ **THIS IS A REACHABLE, SILENT MISCOMPILE, AND IT WAS MEASURED — not a hygiene guard.** A `.text`
// layout map is keyed by NAME and read by `resolveCallFixups`, so a bare last-wins `upsert` does not
// degrade an answer, it BINDS EVERY CALL TO THE OTHER BODY. `function mrt_runtime_init() returns int`
// in an ordinary user program compiled, linked and RAN: the user's body was laid out, the
// hand-assembled panic-runtime chunk of the same name was laid after it, and the program's own call to
// its own function silently reached the runtime chunk instead — **exit 127 where the source says 7**,
// with no diagnostic anywhere. (`mrt_start` was worse only in being loud: it tripped the entry-offset
// guard and PANICKED the compiler on a plain user program, which is not a diagnostic either.)
//
// ⚠ **AND `FunctionNameIndex` CANNOT COVER IT, WHICH IS WHY THE CHECK LIVES HERE.** These names are not
// a module's function names: the chunk list is every Std function PLUS the entry stub PLUS the
// hand-assembled runtime chunks (`mrt_*`, `__gt_*`) PLUS `__symtable` — and the last three are minted at
// the TARGET tier, after the last index any Std pass can build. Which of them exist depends on the target
// and on `RuntimeUsage`, so the set cannot be PREDICTED upstream; it can only be NOTICED here, at the one
// place that decides the layout. That is A1t's lesson (a collision is something to notice, never
// something to predict) applied one tier down.
//
// The count trick is `FunctionNameIndex.create`'s, for its reason: this is called with `laidOut` labels
// already in the map, so the height IS `laidOut + 1` afterwards unless one insert collapsed two names —
// one integer compare, and no second hash probe on a per-chunk loop.
module function recordChunkLabel(labelOffsets FuncLabelOffsetMap, name ByteArray, offset CodeOffset, laidOut ChunkLabelCount, project Project, funcNames FunctionNameIndex) throws CompileError
	labelOffsets.upsert(name, value: offset)

	if labelOffsets.count() != laidOut + 1 'duplicateChunkName'
		try refuseDuplicateChunkName(name, project: project, funcNames: funcNames)
	end 'duplicateChunkName'
end 'recordChunkLabel'

// The DECLARATION that took a name the compiler lays a chunk under. It is the SAME rule and the SAME
// diagnostic as `FunctionNameIndex`'s duplicate refusal (E4015) — a declaration collided with a symbol the
// compiler emits — and it is reported the same way, positioned at the declaration, because the cure is
// the same: rename it. Only the DISCRIMINATOR differs. There both sides are module functions and
// `sourceFilePath` tells the parsed one from the synthesized one; here the compiler's side is not in the
// module at all, so the question is instead "does the Std module define this name, and from a source
// file?" — which the checked index answers in one lookup.
function refuseDuplicateChunkName(name ByteArray, project Project, funcNames FunctionNameIndex) throws CompileError
	let spelled = String.from(name)
	let declared = try funcNames.funcNamed(name) otherwise 'noModuleFunction'
		panic("refuseDuplicateChunkName: two chunks are laid out under '{spelled}' and NEITHER is a Std function, so both were minted at the Target tier — the entry stub, a hand-assembled runtime chunk, or `__symtable`. Each of those is appended at exactly one site, so this is an augmentation that ran twice")
	end 'noModuleFunction'

	if declared.sourceFilePath.isEmpty() 'bothSynthesized'
		panic("refuseDuplicateChunkName: the Std function named '{spelled}' is SYNTHESIZED, and a Target-tier chunk claims the same name — an `install*Runtime` and a hand-assembled chunk both supply this symbol, which is a compiler bug rather than anything a program can cause")
	end 'bothSynthesized'

	reportPositioned(project, code: ErrorCode.declarationTakesCompilerOwnedName, message: "declaration of '{spelled}' collides with a symbol the compiler EMITS into this program, and a program cannot hold two bodies under one `.text` label. The compiler's own definition is the one its emitted code is bound to — the entry stub's calls and the runtime's own call sites — so the declaration is the side that must move: rename it", line: declared.nameLine, column: declared.nameColumn, filePath: declared.sourceFilePath)
	throw CompileError.compileError
end 'refuseDuplicateChunkName'

// Install the OS entry point at the MODULE level. x64-windows prepends `mrt_start` as a module
// function (all-physical registers, its own explicit frame), which concat lays out first; it runs
// AFTER register allocation and prologue/epilogue so the hand-built stub is never reprocessed. This
// pass is WINDOWS-ONLY: the arm64 backend has no module-level entry, because its macOS stub calls
// `_exit` through a GOT import that has no TargetOp channel, so it is hand-assembled as a
// FunctionCodeChunk in the encode phase instead (see `Arm64Backend.buildArm64Backend` /
// `Arm64Runtime`); arm64-linux shares that chunk, differing only in its exit route. So both non-wasi
// non-windows arms answer the same thing and are consolidated into one.
//
// `checkLeaks` is `RuntimeUsage.usesHeap` — whether the MM runtime (and with it `__mm_leak_check`) was
// installed. A program with no allocator cannot leak, so it carries no gate.
//
// `initGlobals` is `RuntimeUsage.usesManagedGlobals` — whether the program declares a managed top-level
// `var`, and so carries `__module_init`/`__maxon_global_cleanup` (P1.7 slice 2a). A program with none
// carries neither call and its emitted stub is byte-identical to before that rung.
//
// `drainGreenThreads` is `RuntimeUsage.usesGt` — whether the green-thread scheduler was installed, and so
// whether `__gt_drain_at_exit` exists to be called. It rides the INSTALLATION bit rather than a probe of the
// module for this file's own recorded reason: dead-function elimination takes installed functions back out,
// so *"does the module still contain X"* is not a question about the runtime X belongs to.
function augmentWithRuntime(module TargetModule, target Target, checkLeaks bool, initGlobals bool, drainGreenThreads bool, debugStream bool)
	match target.os 'os'
		windows then prependEntryStub(module, checkLeaks: checkLeaks, initGlobals: initGlobals, drainGreenThreads: drainGreenThreads, debugStream: debugStream)
		// ⛔ The Linux stub takes no `debugStream`: the producer's three host primitives are x64-windows
		// only and `--debugstream` is REFUSED at the command line on every other target (see
		// `Main.debugStreamSupports`), so this arm can never be reached with the flag on.
		linux then prependLinuxEntryStub(module, checkLeaks: checkLeaks, initGlobals: initGlobals, drainGreenThreads: drainGreenThreads)
		macos then panic("augmentWithRuntime: there is no x64-macOS backend — `buildBackend` dispatches macOS to the arm64 backend, whose entry stub is a hand-assembled chunk (Arm64Backend.buildArm64Backend), not a module function")
		wasi then panic("augmentWithRuntime: wasm32-wasi does not use the x64 backend — the wasm backend synthesizes its own $run entry (buildWasmCoreModule), so this module-level pass is never reached for it")
	end 'os'
end 'augmentWithRuntime'

// Build `mrt_start` and lay it out FIRST so its `.text` offset is the PE entry
// point. The stub bridges Maxon's custom ABI (primary return in R8) to Win64's
// ExitProcess (UINT arg in ECX):
//   push rbp           open the saved-RBP chain (see below)
//   mov rbp, rsp       … so a backtrace can name mrt_start and then stop
//   sub rsp, 0x20      reserve shadow space (rsp is already 16-aligned by the push)
//   call mrt_runtime_init   install the fault handler BEFORE any user code can fault
//   [call __module_init]    fill every EAGER managed global's .data slot (P1.7 slice 2a)
//   call main          main returns its ExitCode in R8 (custom ABI)
//   [global cleanup]   mov rcx, r8 ; call __maxon_global_cleanup  -> the same code back in R8
//   mov ecx, r8d       hand R8's low 32 bits to ExitProcess's ECX arg (Win64 ABI)
//   call [ExitProcess] terminate the process with that code
//   add rsp, 0x20      release the frame — dead, but the mirror must be total
//   pop rbp            restore the caller's frame pointer
//   ret                dead (ExitProcess never returns); kept for well-formedness
//
// This stub is built AFTER `insertPrologueEpilogue`, so it does not inherit that pass's
// frame and must lay its own. It builds the SAME frame that pass builds — `push rbp` /
// `mov rbp, rsp` through the shared `framePointerSetupOp`, so the chain's defining
// instruction is spelled in one place — because `mrt_start` is the frame the chain ENDS
// at: `main`'s saved rbp points here, and a walk that arrives with no frame to read stops
// one frame short of the `in mrt_start` every backtrace in the corpus expects. Its own
// saved rbp is whatever the loader left, and the walk's `.text` bound is what discards it.
function prependEntryStub(module TargetModule, checkLeaks bool, initGlobals bool, drainGreenThreads bool, debugStream bool)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(EntryPointName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 0, paramTypes: StdTypeArray.create(), returnLine: 0)
	var block = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")

	module.appendOp(block, op: TargetOp.pushReg(X64Register.rbp))
	module.appendOp(block, op: framePointerSetupOp())
	module.appendOp(block, op: TargetOp.prologue(EntryStubFrameBytes))
	module.appendOp(block, op: TargetOp.callDirect(RuntimeInitName))
	// ⭐ **THE PRODUCER ATTACHES BEFORE `__module_init`, WHICH IS THE FIRST THING THAT CAN ALLOCATE.** An
	// eager managed global's box is a real allocation and belongs in the trace; attaching after it would
	// silently drop exactly the allocations a program makes before `main`. It takes no argument, returns
	// nothing and has no live value to preserve across it, so it is a bare call — `__module_init`'s shape.
	appendDebugStreamCall(module, block: block, debugStream: debugStream, callee: DsInitName)
	appendModuleInitCall(module, block: block, initGlobals: initGlobals)
	module.appendOp(block, op: TargetOp.callDirect(UserEntryName.toByteArray()))
	appendGlobalCleanupGate(module, block: block, initGlobals: initGlobals)
	appendGreenThreadDrainGate(module, block: block, drainGreenThreads: drainGreenThreads)

	// ⭐ THE LEAK GATE (P1.0r) — the one seam on the exit path, and the reason `__mm_decref` has a
	// reason to be right at all. `main` has returned its ExitCode in R8; `__mm_leak_check` takes
	// that code and gives back either the same code (nothing outstanding) or 101 (something was
	// allocated and never freed). Every program that touches the heap therefore checks itself, and
	// a missing decref is a FAILING TEST rather than an invisible one.
	//
	// It sits between `call main` and the ExitProcess bridge because that is the only point where
	// the program is finished AND has not yet exited. It is skipped entirely for a program with no
	// allocator, which cannot leak — there is nothing to count.
	//
	// The argument goes in RCX and the result comes back in R8: `__mm_leak_check` is an ordinary
	// Maxon-ABI function (arg 0 = rcx, primary return = r8), not a Win64 import.
	appendLeakGate(module, block: block, checkLeaks: checkLeaks)

	// ⚠ AFTER the leak gate, and it must be: `__ds_shutdown` clears the segment's producer-alive flag,
	// and the leak check is the last thing that can still free a box — so the trace's final events happen
	// above this line. It preserves nothing because there is nothing to preserve: R8 holds the exit code
	// and this call, like `__ds_init`, is a bare void one that never touches it.
	appendDebugStreamCall(module, block: block, debugStream: debugStream, callee: DsShutdownName)

	module.appendOp(block, op: TargetOp.movReg32Reg32(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rcx), srcReg: x64PhysReg(X64Register.r8)))
	module.appendOp(block, op: TargetOp.iatCall(iatSlotIndex(IatSlot.ExitProcess)))
	module.appendOp(block, op: TargetOp.epilogue(EntryStubFrameBytes))
	module.appendOp(block, op: TargetOp.popReg(X64Register.rbp))
	module.setTerminator(block, op: TargetOp.ret)

	prependFunction(module, func: func)
end 'prependEntryStub'

// ⭐ THE LEAK GATE (P1.0r) — the one seam on the exit path, shared by both entry stubs. `main` has
// returned its ExitCode in R8; `__mm_leak_check` takes that code (arg 0 in RCX, custom Maxon ABI) and
// gives back either the same code (nothing outstanding) or 101 (something was allocated and never
// freed), in R8. The value flow r8 -> rcx -> (adjusted) r8 is identical on Windows and Linux — only the
// surrounding process-exit primitive differs — so the gate is written once here. It is skipped entirely
// for a program with no allocator (`checkLeaks` false), which cannot leak; every program that touches
// the heap therefore checks itself, and a missing decref is a FAILING TEST rather than an invisible one.
function appendLeakGate(module TargetModule, block IrBlock, checkLeaks bool)
	if checkLeaks 'leakGate'
		appendExitCodePassThroughCall(module, block: block, callee: MmLeakCheckName)
	end 'leakGate'
end 'appendLeakGate'

// ⭐ **FILL EVERY EAGER managed global's `.data` SLOT BEFORE `main`** (P1.7 slice 2a) — a `static`
// member's is filled on first access instead (see `ModuleInit`) — shared by both entry
// stubs, and the FIRST thing after the runtime init so a global is live for the whole of user code.
// `__module_init` takes no argument and returns nothing; there is no live value to preserve across it
// (`main` has not run), so it is a bare call. Emitted only for a program that has one (`initGlobals`).
// The producer's two bare calls — attach and detach — emitted only into a `--debugstream` build. One
// function for both because they are one shape and one gate: a stub built without the flag is
// byte-identical to what it was before this file learned the flag existed.
function appendDebugStreamCall(module TargetModule, block IrBlock, debugStream bool, callee ByteArray)
	if debugStream 'tracedBuild'
		module.appendOp(block, op: TargetOp.callDirect(callee))
	end 'tracedBuild'
end 'appendDebugStreamCall'

function appendModuleInitCall(module TargetModule, block IrBlock, initGlobals bool)
	if initGlobals 'moduleInit'
		module.appendOp(block, op: TargetOp.callDirect(ModuleInitName))
	end 'moduleInit'
end 'appendModuleInitCall'

// ⭐ **RELEASE EVERY MANAGED GLOBAL AFTER `main`, BEFORE THE LEAK GATE** (P1.7 slice 2a) — shared by both
// entry stubs. The ORDER is the whole point: `__mm_leak_check` counts what is still allocated, so a global
// released after it would read as a leak (exit 101) and one never released would read as one too. That is
// exactly the property `static-variables.md`'s `top-level-var-string-mutate-cross-function` gates.
//
// It rides `appendExitCodePassThroughCall` because `__maxon_global_cleanup` is shaped like
// `__mm_leak_check` deliberately: `main`'s ExitCode is the one live value on this path, and a void callee
// would clobber it in R8 (see `ModuleInit.buildGlobalCleanup`).
function appendGlobalCleanupGate(module TargetModule, block IrBlock, initGlobals bool)
	if initGlobals 'globalCleanup'
		appendExitCodePassThroughCall(module, block: block, callee: GlobalCleanupName)
	end 'globalCleanup'
end 'appendGlobalCleanupGate'

// ⭐⭐ **RUN THE SCHEDULER OUT AND JOIN ITS WORKER Ms, AFTER `main` AND BEFORE THE LEAK GATE (SV1)** —
// shared by both entry stubs. The ORDER is the whole point, twice over:
//
//   • **AFTER the global cleanup**, because a released managed global may be the last owner of a service
//     handle, and dropping it is what lets that service's green thread finish.
//   • **BEFORE `__mm_leak_check`**, because a worker M's `__gt_reclaim` steps the very counters that gate
//     reads. Un-joined, the two race and a clean program reports 101 intermittently — and a green thread
//     that had not finished yet would report 75 for work that was merely still running.
//
// It rides `appendExitCodePassThroughCall` because `__gt_drain_at_exit` is shaped like `__mm_leak_check`
// deliberately: `main`'s ExitCode is the one live value on this path, and a void callee would clobber it in
// R8. A program with no scheduler carries neither the call nor the function.
function appendGreenThreadDrainGate(module TargetModule, block IrBlock, drainGreenThreads bool)
	if drainGreenThreads 'greenThreadDrain'
		appendExitCodePassThroughCall(module, block: block, callee: GtDrainAtExitName)
	end 'greenThreadDrain'
end 'appendGreenThreadDrainGate'

// Route `main`'s ExitCode (R8, the custom Maxon ABI's primary return) through `callee` and back into R8:
// `mov rcx, r8` puts it in argument register 0, and the callee's own primary return lands where the next
// stage of the exit path expects it. The ONE spelling of that value flow, shared by the leak gate and the
// global-cleanup gate — two copies would be two chances to drop the exit code on the floor, silently.
function appendExitCodePassThroughCall(module TargetModule, block IrBlock, callee ByteArray)
	module.appendOp(block, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rcx), srcReg: x64PhysReg(X64Register.r8)))
	module.appendOp(block, op: TargetOp.callDirect(callee))
end 'appendExitCodePassThroughCall'

// Build the x64-LINUX entry stub `mrt_start` as a module function and lay it out FIRST — its `.text`
// offset is the ELF entry point (`writeElf`'s `entryPoint = textVAddr + mainCallFixupOffset`, and concat
// asserts the stub leads `.text`). The Linux twin of `prependEntryStub`, and CARRYING NO FRAME, for
// reasons that all point the same way:
//
//   call mrt_runtime_init installs the SIGFPE handler (rt_sigaction) — appendX64LinuxRuntimeChunks
//   [call __module_init]  fill every EAGER managed global's .data slot (P1.7 slice 2a)
//   call main            main returns its ExitCode in R8 (custom ABI)
//   [global cleanup]     mov rcx, r8 ; call __maxon_global_cleanup  ->  the same code back in R8
//   [leak gate]          mov rcx, r8 ; call __mm_leak_check  ->  adjusted code / 101 in R8
//   mov edi, r8d         hand R8's low 32 bits to exit_group's rdi (Linux syscall arg 0)
//   x64Syscall(exit_group)   mov eax, 231 ; syscall  — terminates the process
//   ret                  dead (exit_group never returns); kept as the block's terminator for
//                        well-formedness, exactly as the Windows stub's trailing `ret` is.
//
// ⚠ NO `push rbp`, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE — IT IS THE ALIGNMENT. The Linux kernel enters `_start` with
// rsp ≡ 0 (mod 16) (argc at `[rsp]`, NO return address), where the Windows loader enters ≡ 8 (as if via
// `call`). So `call main` from the kernel's alignment lands `main` at rsp ≡ 8 (mod 16) — exactly what a
// shv2 function expects — with no push to invert the parity. The Windows stub's `push rbp` (which opens
// the backtrace chain) would flip it the WRONG way here and misalign `main`. And the stub needs no
// frame: the SIGFPE backtrace chains through MAIN's frame (main's saved rbp is the kernel-zeroed rbp
// this frameless stub left, so the trace is `in main` -> `in mrt_start` -> ends), there is no Win64
// shadow space to reserve (raw syscalls carry no imports, and shv2 functions spill to their OWN frames),
// and exit_group never returns. The kernel zeroes rbp at entry (SysV ABI), so the saved-rbp chain
// terminates cleanly without this stub setting one up.
function prependLinuxEntryStub(module TargetModule, checkLeaks bool, initGlobals bool, drainGreenThreads bool)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(EntryPointName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 0, paramTypes: StdTypeArray.create(), returnLine: 0)
	var block = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")

	// Install the SIGFPE fault handler (rt_sigaction) before any user code runs, exactly as the Windows
	// stub calls `mrt_runtime_init` first. `RuntimeInitName` resolves to the LINUX init
	// `appendX64LinuxRuntimeChunks` appended; it runs from the kernel's 16-aligned entry and returns with
	// rsp restored, so the following `call main` still lands main at rsp≡8 (mod 16).
	module.appendOp(block, op: TargetOp.callDirect(RuntimeInitName))
	appendModuleInitCall(module, block: block, initGlobals: initGlobals)
	module.appendOp(block, op: TargetOp.callDirect(UserEntryName.toByteArray()))
	appendGlobalCleanupGate(module, block: block, initGlobals: initGlobals)
	appendGreenThreadDrainGate(module, block: block, drainGreenThreads: drainGreenThreads)
	appendLeakGate(module, block: block, checkLeaks: checkLeaks)

	// ExitCode (R8, custom ABI) -> EDI (Linux syscall arg 0). A 32-bit move, exactly as the Windows stub
	// hands R8 to ECX for ExitProcess: an exit status is the low 8 bits, and the zero-extending `mov r32`
	// clears the high half.
	module.appendOp(block, op: TargetOp.movReg32Reg32(x64PhysReg(X64Register.rdi), srcReg: x64PhysReg(X64Register.r8)))
	module.appendOp(block, op: TargetOp.x64Syscall(X64LinuxSyscallExitGroup))
	module.setTerminator(block, op: TargetOp.ret)

	prependFunction(module, func: func)
end 'prependLinuxEntryStub'

// Move `func` to the front of the module's function list so it lays out first in
// `.text`. Block refs are flat `module.blocks` indices, so reordering the
// function list never invalidates them.
function prependFunction(module TargetModule, func IrFunction)
	var reordered = IrFunctionArray.create()
	reordered.push(func)
	for existing in module.functions 'eachFunc'
		reordered.push(existing)
	end 'eachFunc'
	module.functions = reordered
end 'prependFunction'

// Write the CodeResult to a runnable executable, dispatched on target OS: an x64-windows PE, an
// arm64-macOS Mach-O, an arm64-linux static ELF, or a wasm32-wasi component (`writeWasm`).
// Each writer lives beside the format it emits; the wasm one takes the core module bytes rather than
// the whole CodeResult because building a component is a multi-step, multi-file job it owns end to
// end (see its header).
export function writeExecutable(codeResult CodeResult, target Target, outputPath FilePath)
	match target.os 'os'
		windows then writePe(outputPath, codeResult: codeResult, target: target)
		macos then writeMachO(outputPath, codeResult: codeResult, target: target)
		linux then writeElf(outputPath, codeResult: codeResult, target: target)
		wasi then writeWasm(outputPath, coreModule: codeResult.wasmCoreModule)
	end 'os'
end 'writeExecutable'
